  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The Four Corners High/Upper Level \nTrough (4C/ULT) setup that has controlled the weather pattern over\nthe SW CONUS recently has begun to break down Saturday as the HPC\ntreks eastward into eastern NM. Enough residual monsoon moisture \nand remnants of TS Iselle are providing sufficient moisture \navailability for strong to severe thunderstorm activity over \neastern and east-central NV Saturday afternoon. A mid-level jet \nover northern UT/southern WY is generating ample shear and forcing\nover eastern NV. Instability already in place, evidenced by early\nmorning storm activity, is being enhanced by surface heating this\nafternoon. The results will be strong thunderstorms developing \nover southern Eureka, White Pine, and southeastern Elko Counties \nthis afternoon. Main threats will be surface winds gusts of 60-85 \nmph, hail up to 1.25 inches in diameter, and potential for \nlocalized flooding due to excessive rainfall. These threats will \ncontinue through early Saturday evening.\n\nThe region dries out from west to east overnight tonight with\nclearing skies and overnight lows in the mid to upper 40s. The ULT\nover the NW CONUS takes over as the main driver of the pattern\nover the western CONUS headed into next week. This will result in\na series of shortwaves riding the parent trough flow across the\nGreat Basin. The first of said waves will traverse from central CA\ninto NV on Monday morning. Eastern NV will be in its downstream\nflow where enough moisture and forcing will be present to touch \noff showers and thunderstorms along the eastern border Monday \nafternoon. Rainfall accumulations of up to 0.10 of an inch are\npossible especially over eastern Elko County. Temperatures over\nthe area this week will be regulated by repeated bouts of cloud\ncover and precipitation chances keeping afternoon highs close to\nseasonal norms. \n\n\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continues for\nshower and thunderstorm activity that could produce excessive\nrainfall and severe weather Saturday afternoon. Moderate\nconfidence remains for a cooling trend into next week. Low\nconfidence exists for precipitation chances and amounts Monday\nafternoon. No major grids changes needed at this time.",
